brainLessons.push(...[["NOTICE THE MENTAL FILTER", "When one awkward moment dominates your account of a day, other information can disappear. A fair description includes what went well, what was difficult and what is still unresolved. This is about widening the evidence, rather than insisting on a cheerful conclusion.", "A meeting can contain an unclear answer and a useful decision. Neither erases the other.", ["Describe one difficult moment.", "Add two other concrete observations from the day.", "Write a fuller account that includes all three."], "Which part of today am I leaving out?", "Reframing unhelpful thoughts · NHS", "https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/mental-wellbeing-tips/self-help-cbt-techniques/reframing-unhelpful-thoughts/"], ["GIVE A WORRY A PLACE", "A short, planned period for writing down worries can offer an alternative to repeatedly revisiting them throughout the day. Use that time to distinguish practical problems from uncertainties you cannot settle. If it does not help, adapt the approach rather than treating it as a rule.", "You note a concern during breakfast and return to it at a chosen time, instead of reopening it with every notification.", ["Write down one worry.", "Choose a short time to consider it.", "At that time, decide whether it has a practical next step."], "What could I put down for now?", "Tackling your worries · NHS", "https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/mental-wellbeing-tips/self-help-cbt-techniques/tackling-your-worries/"], ["RECALL BEFORE READING AGAIN", "Rereading can make a sentence feel familiar. Trying to recall it first shows which parts you can produce yourself. Check the answer afterward and correct it. A brief attempt is useful even when the result is incomplete.", "Before today’s Portuguese lesson, say yesterday’s phrase without looking. Then compare the actual wording.", ["Choose one phrase or idea.", "Try to recall it without looking.", "Check and correct your answer."], "What is familiar but not yet easy to recall?", "Retrieval practice · educator guide", "https://www.retrievalpractice.org/retrievalpractice"], ["CHOOSE A CUE YOU CAN NOTICE", "An if–then plan needs a recognizable situation. A cue such as finishing breakfast is easier to identify than feeling motivated. Choose a small action you can actually do when that situation arrives.", "After closing your workday, you put one item in its place before starting your evening.", ["Name a reliable event in your day.", "Pair it with one small action.", "Try it and check whether you noticed the cue."], "Which daily moment could remind me?", "Implementation intentions · National Cancer Institute", "https://cancercontrol.cancer.gov/brp/research/constructs/implementation-intentions"], ["ASK THE FRIEND QUESTION", "When checking an unhelpful thought, consider what you would say to a friend with the same evidence. You may notice uncertainty or context that your first judgment omitted. Keep your response realistic: kindness does not require denying a mistake.", "If a friend missed a deadline, you might help them choose a next step instead of declaring them unreliable forever.", ["Write one harsh judgment.", "Imagine a friend described the same event.", "Give yourself the balanced response you would offer them."], "What would I tell a friend in this situation?", "Reframing unhelpful thoughts · NHS", "https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/mental-wellbeing-tips/self-help-cbt-techniques/reframing-unhelpful-thoughts/"], ["SCHEDULE THE ACTION, RELEASE THE LOOP", "A practical worry sometimes needs action later, rather than action now. Write down what you will do and when. If the same worry returns, remind yourself of the plan and return to the present task.", "You cannot call an office after it closes. Put the call and its purpose on tomorrow’s list.", ["Identify what can be done.", "Choose a realistic time.", "Record the next step where you will find it."], "What needs a plan rather than another round of thinking?", "Tackling your worries · NHS", "https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/mental-wellbeing-tips/self-help-cbt-techniques/tackling-your-worries/"], ["REVIEW BY TEACHING IT BACK", "Explaining an idea without looking is a form of retrieval. It also reveals missing connections. Keep your explanation short, check it against the source, and revise anything inaccurate. The goal is clearer understanding, not a polished performance.", "Explain the difference between a fact and an interpretation in two sentences, then compare it with the lesson.", ["Pick an idea from this week.", "Explain it aloud without your notes.", "Check one detail and improve the explanation."], "Which idea could I explain to someone else?", "Retrieval practice · educator guide", "https://www.retrievalpractice.org/retrievalpractice"]]);
